course: dictionary
lesson: 13311
title: A formal word: grievously
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Grievously» is a formal word. It tells you how something happened, and it means very seriously or badly. You will meet it in history books, war stories, the Bible and news reports, but people in everyday talk usually say «badly» or «seriously» instead.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «The soldier was grievously wounded in the battle.» Wounded means hurt, and a battle is dangerous, so grievously must mean very seriously. Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap in your meaning and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: The word can also describe harm to feelings. «She felt grievously wronged when her friend told lies about her.» Wronged means treated unfairly, and the sentence shows that the harm hurt her deeply. So grievously can mean in a way that causes great sorrow or harm.
fact: Grievously is an adverb, and it describes how something was done or happened.
fact: «Grievously» means very seriously or badly, as in «grievously wounded».
fact: It can also mean in a way that causes great sorrow or harm, as in «grievously wronged».
fact: It is a formal word, so you will read it more often than you will hear it in playground talk.
fact: Good everyday swaps are «badly» and «seriously», but they are not always as strong as «grievously».
exercise: «The soldier was grievously wounded in the battle.» What does grievously mean here? | Very quietly | Very quickly | Very happily | *Very seriously or badly
exercise: «She felt grievously wronged when her friend told lies about her.» What does grievously suggest? | Very loudly, so everyone could hear | In a happy and cheerful way | Quickly and quietly | *In a way that causes great sorrow or harm
exercise: Which word could replace grievously in «He was grievously hurt in the fall»? | quietly | gently | happily | *badly
test: «The news report said the climber was grievously injured on the mountain.» What does grievously mean? | *Very seriously or badly | Slowly and carefully | Happily, with a smile | Lightly and without any harm
test: Maya says, «I was grievously mistaken about the time.» Which word is closest in meaning to grievously here? | *Seriously | Politely | Slightly | Quickly
exam: «The old king was grievously wronged by a man he trusted.» What does grievously mean here? | Very seriously, causing great sorrow or harm | *In a way that causes great sorrow or harm | Very happily | Only just
